<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[Placeholder]</span></p>
<p>I enjoy the theme you have going on, but I'm concerned about the vagueness of the idea. You mentioned that the owner committed suicide, but in your reply to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6391595&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1604624542" alt="Quadraginta" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6391595)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;">Quadraginta</a></span>, you instead said that the owner was influenced to commit suicide because of an undiscovered anomaly.</p>
<p>Firstly, that just doesn't scratch the itch, you know? It's kinda lame that we don't know what anomaly did it and what events led up to her death so it ends up coming off as lazy writing. The SCP being the cause of the owner's death seems very hamfisted in, and in this context, it looks like this situation is fabricated for the sole reason of making the pink plushie hate other anomalies. I recommend you think up of a more well-thought-out backstory for the owner's suicide, otherwise readers will be left dissatisfied that the reason for the owner's death was due to some undiscovered anomaly.</p>
<p>Play more into the pink plushie's hopelessness as it sees their owner spiral further into depression. It doesn't need to hate or kill other anomalies, it just needs to be broken because it's helpless, because it couldn't do anything for the owner despite them going through so much. Then the regret it feels manifests into the ropes and yada yada. You get the rest of the flow!</p>
<p>One last worry is that since this is your first SCP, writing about suicide is really hard, and if you phrase it wrongly anywhere in your article, the message will come off as insincere and even quite distasteful. I'm just saying tread carefully when you're handling a sensitive topic like this. Contact me again if you want another review.</p>

							</div>

								
