<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Yeet!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>After reviewing <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/forbiddenquest" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6394686); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6394686&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605383646" alt="forbiddenquest" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6394686)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/forbiddenquest" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6394686); return false;">forbiddenquest</a></span>'s take on this concept and reviewing my own previous crit, I do agree with him that it's pretty good now, but I'm also still worried about how you'll present it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the test logs mainly shows the characters powers, how strong or weak it is. And its reaction to it owners absence and it feeling towards being hold in a cell. since this SCP is Sapient and has thoughts.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This can be a good way to show the grief it feels from losing its owner, but focusing on how strong it is could potentially detract from the emotional part and make it seem more&#8230;idk, video game-ish? Perhaps showing its anomalous abilities in an incident report could be better. <em>Perhaps.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Journal Entry is only going to be the small part of the article. the Journal parts will only show the event leading up the owners death.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is good to hear, but it'll be a bit of a balancing act to keep it from being too expository/info-dumpy as well as too long or too short, even if it is just a small part.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the interview and audio log would be main way of showing the past. The SCP itself is able to communicate when wanted to, along with Owner mother giving in the couple of interview.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is my favorite part, because this has so much emotional potential. The mother interviews her dead daughter's now sapient doll&#8230;imagine the conversation the two could have. They could laugh together over what they loved most about her. Cry together on how they both lost a precious family member. In a twisted way, this doll could even fill a tiny portion of the hole in the mother's heart, [pseudo-replacing the daughter she lost.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>i also thinking of mail communication, showing bits and pieces of who bought the toy in the first place and where they bought it. but also Leaving the imagination to the reader. (I’m still haven’t decided yet)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure if this is even necessary tbh.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation is more interested in how this toy came to life and its personality,and mainly it end goal.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You mention an end goal, but would it even have one? Or need one? As far as I've seen so far, its reason for existing is that it grew to life from grief and misses its owner.</p>
<p>All in all, I do like everything you've presented so far, but&#8230;hm&#8230;you have a lot going for you right now. Like a <em>lot</em>.</p>
<p>Note, I am a very nitpicky person and I don't like to give out tentative greenlights.</p>
<p>I apologize if this isn't the answer you're looking for, but I'll pass giving a greenlight for the time being.</p>
<p>I <em>can't</em> quite explain the feeling I'm having, but I like to follow my intuition in regards to things like this.</p>
<p>Like, the <strong>Central Narrative</strong> portion says the doll will be falling apart and be filled with sadness even before this all occurs</p>
<p>Here's a suggestion that someone gave me for my first SCP a while back. Why not an epistolary format? Notes, letters, videos and such?</p>
<p>I don't see the girl interacting with her own doll anywhere in your listed formats. It's all post-mortem.</p>
<p>I think I'd like to see that. It doesn't even have to be that much. Like in Stephen King's Under the Dome, Howie has a short interaction with his wife before he dies and the wife's grief was palpable for the next 1000+ pages of the story, I'd like to see something between the girl and her doll, which none of your list formats have shown thus far.</p>
<p>Either way, I apologize if this disappoints.</p>
<p>I recommend seeking out another reviewer on the <a href="/butterfly-squad-roster">+Butterfly squad roster</a>. I've looked at this twice and I think a fresh perspective would help.</p>
<p>I hope I didn't disappoint too much and I bid you a good day!</p>
</div>
</div>
